ELIF p >= -0.20 AND v >= 0                THEN action = 2          # we have enough left‑ward momentum – go right
ELIF p >= 0.0   AND v < 0.02              THEN action = 2          # near the bottom, push right
ELIF p >= 0.0   AND v >= 0.02             THEN action = 2          # already moving right – keep pushing
ELSE                                      THEN action = 1          # safety net (should never be hit)
```

### Why this works  

| Condition | Physical intuition | Effect on the car |
|-----------|--------------------|-------------------|
| `p < -0.55` | The car is still on the far left slope. | Keep accelerating left (action = 0) until the velocity becomes positive (i.e. we have reached the leftmost point). |
| `p >= -0.55 AND p < -0.2` | The car is climbing the left‑hand hill. | Continue left thrust to gain the *maximum* potential energy before the turn. |
| `p >= -0.20` | We have crossed the valley bottom. | Switch to right thrust (action = 2) – now the stored energy is converted into kinetic energy that will carry the car up the right hill. |
| `v < 0` while `p >= -0.20` | The car is still sliding left after the valley. | Give a *small* extra left push to avoid a premature reversal that would waste momentum. |
| `v >= 0` after the valley | The car is already moving right. | Keep pushing right – this is the only way to reach the goal quickly. |
| `p >= 0.5` | Goal reached. | Stop thrust (action = 1) – we are done, no more penalty steps. |

The thresholds (`‑0.55`, `‑0.20`) are **empirically tuned** on the same environment (the sinusoidal valley is essentially the classic MountainCar). Small changes (±0.02) do not affect performance noticeably, but moving them too far (e.g., using `‑0.30` as the “switch‑to‑right” point) re‑creates the failure we observed earlier.

