| **Policy 1 (the “old” one)** | **‑200** (the maximum episode length) | **No** – the car never left the left‑hand side of the valley. | The rule set kept pushing the car **right** as soon as the velocity became slightly positive, even when the car was still far from the bottom of the valley (p ≈ ‑0.3). The car therefore never built enough kinetic energy to climb the right hill. |
| **Policy 2 (the “current” one)** | **‑163.7** (≈ 36 % faster) | **No** – the car still fell short of p = 0.5, but it managed to move far enough to the right before the episode timed‑out. | The logic distinguishes three zones (far‑left, middle, near‑right) and only starts accelerating right **after** the velocity has become positive **and** the car has passed the bottom (p ≈ ‑0.55). This allowed the car to gather a larger momentum, but the thresholds are still a little too conservative – the car spends many steps coasting at low speed around the bottom (p ≈ ‑0.8 … ‑1.2) before it finally flips to “accelerate‑right”. |

**Key take‑aways**

1. **Momentum matters** – the car must first be driven hard to the left until it reaches the deepest part of the valley (≈ ‑0.6 m). Only then should we switch to “right‑acceleration” and let gravity add to the speed.
2. **Velocity‑based switching** – using only position thresholds leads to premature right‑thrusts; a small positive velocity at a still‑negative position is not enough to guarantee that the car will climb the hill.
3. **Avoid unnecessary “do‑nothing” actions** – every step with action = 1 costs a –1 reward without contributing to momentum. The best policies keep the engine on (0 or 2) except when a switch is required.

