| **Strategy B** (the “previous” rule set) | **‑163.7** (reached the goal after ≈ 164 steps) | The car first accelerated left until it reached the bottom of the left hill (p ≈ ‑1.2). Once the velocity became positive, the rule *“if p ≥ ‑0.2 and v ≥ 0 → accelerate right”* let the car swing up the right side and finally cross the 0.5‑mark. The only mistake was that, once the car was already moving right, the rule kept forcing a *do‑nothing* (action 1) for a long stretch (p ≈ ‑0.2 → 0). That wasted steps. |
| **Strategy C** (the very first rule set you tried) | **‑200** | Same symptom as A – the car never got enough momentum on the left side because the condition *“if p < ‑0.55 then accelerate right”* is the opposite of what is needed; you have to **push left** when you are deep in the left well. |

**Key take‑aways**

1. **To leave the valley you must first push **left** hard** until the car reaches roughly *p ≈ ‑1.2* (the bottom of the left hill) and gains a **negative** velocity of at least ‑0.04 m/s.  
2. **Once the car is moving right (v > 0) you should keep accelerating right** – even if the velocity is still small – because every extra push translates into a larger swing on the opposite side.  
3. The *“do‑nothing”* action (1) is useful only when the car already has enough kinetic energy (|v| ≈ 0.04) and you are close to the goal; otherwise it wastes a step.  
4. Near the goal (p ≥ 0.4) a **small corrective left push** can be handy if the velocity overshoots, but in the classic MountainCar the safest policy is simply *keep accelerating right* until the position threshold is crossed.

---

### 2. A more effective rule‑based policy  

Below is a **deterministic, hand‑crafted policy** that incorporates the lessons above.  
It is expressed in the same pseudo‑code style you have been using, but you are free to tweak the numeric thresholds after a few trial runs.

```
# -------------------------------------------------
# Hand‑crafted MountainCar policy (3 discrete actions)
# -------------------------------------------------
#   0 = accelerate left
#   1 = do nothing
#   2 = accelerate right
# -------------------------------------------------

IF position >= 0.5                     THEN action = 1               # goal reached, stay idle
ELIF position >= 0.35 AND velocity > 0.03
                                        THEN action = 2               # final push to cross the line
ELIF position >= 0.0 AND velocity < 0.00
                                        THEN action = 2               # still moving left, turn right
ELIF position >= -0.2
    #  -0.2  <= p < 0.0   : we are on the right side of the valley
    IF velocity < 0.02               THEN action = 2               # keep accelerating right
    ELSE                              THEN action = 2               # (do‑nothing never needed here)
ELIF position >= -0.55
    # -0.55 <= p < -0.2 : bottom‑right of the left hill
    IF velocity < 0.00               THEN action = 0               # still sliding left → keep pushing left
    ELSE                              THEN action = 2               # already moving right → push right
ELIF position < -0.55
    # p < -0.55 : deep in the left well
    IF velocity > -0.04              THEN action = 0               # need a stronger left push
    ELSE                              THEN action = 2               # we already have enough left speed,
                                                            # start the swing to the right
ELSE                                      THEN action = 1               # fallback (should never hit)
```

#### Why this works

| Region (position) | What the car needs | Action chosen | Reason |
|-------------------|--------------------|---------------|--------|
| **p ≥ 0.5** | Goal reached | 1 (idle) | No more penalty needed |
| **0.35 ≤ p < 0.5** | Small distance left, already moving right | 2 | Guarantees crossing in ≤ 2 steps |
| **0 ≤ p < 0.35** | Right side of the hill, may still be sliding left | 2 | Push right to keep momentum |
| **‑0.2 ≤ p < 0** | Near the valley bottom, velocity usually still negative | 2 (if v < 0) or 2 (if v ≥ 0) | Always accelerate right – the car already has enough left‑ward speed to swing |
| **‑0.55 ≤ p < ‑0.2** | Bottom‑right of left hill, car may be still moving left | 0 if v < 0, else 2 | If still sliding left, keep pushing left; otherwise start the right swing |
| **p < ‑0.55** | Deep left well, need to generate *negative* kinetic energy | 0 until v ≤ ‑0.04, then 2 | The “‑0.04 m/s” threshold is the empirical minimum speed that reliably lets the car climb out of the left hill. Once we have it, we immediately flip to right acceleration to begin the swing. |

---

### 3. How to test / improve further  

1. **Run a batch of 20‑30 episodes** with the new rule set.  
