### 1️⃣ What the experiments have taught us  

| Strategy | Mean episode reward | What we observed |
|----------|--------------------|------------------|
| **S‑1 (current)** – many nested *if‑else* clauses | **‑200.0** | The car never left the bottom of the valley.  Most of the time we kept applying the *right* thrust (`action = 2`) even when the velocity was still negative, so the car kept sliding back to the left and the episode ran out of steps. |
| **S‑2 (previous)** – “if‑else” with a single left‑push region | **‑200.0** | The rule `position < -0.55 → action = 2` (right) is the opposite of what the classic Mountain‑Car solution needs.  We were trying to go right before we had enough momentum, so the car got stuck near the centre (≈ ‑0.33 m). |
| **S‑3 (best so far)** – piece‑wise policy that finally reaches the goal | **‑163.7** | This policy recognises the *left‑push* region (`p < -0.55`) and drives left there, then switches to right thrust once the car has crossed the “mid‑valley” (`p ≥ -0.2`).  It does manage to climb the right hill, but it spends a long time cruising around `p ≈ –1.2 … –1.0` before the final right push, so the episode is still relatively long. |

**Key take‑aways**

1. **You must first drive left hard enough to build kinetic energy.**  
   The classic “Mountain‑Car” solution pushes left until the position is roughly ≤ ‑0.55 (or until the velocity is sufficiently negative).  

2. **After you have enough left‑hand momentum, you must switch to right thrust *as soon as* the velocity becomes positive and the car is past the bottom of the valley.**  
   Keeping the right thrust while the velocity is still negative (or while the car is still left of the bottom) wastes the energy you just earned.

3. **Very fine‑grained thresholds (e.g. `velocity < 0.02` vs `velocity < 0.03`) do not change the outcome much; the decisive factor is the *region* (left‑push vs right‑push) and the sign of the velocity.**  

The best‑performing strategy already respects the two‑phase idea, but it lingers too long in the “mid‑valley” (`‑0.55 ≤ p < –0.2`) with a blanket `action = 0`.  In many of those steps the velocity is already positive, so we are actually *braking* ourselves.

---

### 2️⃣ Revised policy – “Two‑phase Momentum Policy”

Below is a compact rule‑set that keeps the two phases explicit and adds a **velocity‑sign check** inside the transition region.  It is written in the same pseudo‑code style you have been using.

```text
IF position >= 0.5                         THEN action = 1               # goal reached → coast
ELIF position < -0.55 AND velocity < 0     THEN action = 0               # Phase‑1: push left hard
ELIF position < -0.55 AND velocity >= 0    THEN action = 2               # overshoot → start right thrust

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Transition zone: we are between the leftmost push region and the valley bottom
ELIF position >= -0.55 AND position < -0.2
    IF velocity < 0                         THEN action = 0               # still moving left → keep left
    ELSE                                    THEN action = 2               # velocity already positive → start right

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Near the bottom (‑0.2 ≤ position < 0)
ELIF position >= -0.2 AND position < 0
    IF velocity < 0                         THEN action = 0               # still sliding left → keep left
    ELSE                                    THEN action = 2               # moving right → keep accelerating right

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Slightly past the bottom but not yet on the hill (0 ≤ position < 0.5)
ELIF position >= 0 AND position < 0.5
    IF velocity < 0.02                      THEN action = 2               # need a little extra push
    ELSE                                    THEN action = 1               # coast (we are already climbing)

ELSE                                            action = 1               # safety fallback
```

#### Why this should improve the score

| Region | What the rule does | Why it helps |
|--------|--------------------|--------------|
| **p < ‑0.55 & v < 0** | Full left thrust (`0`) | Guarantees we reach the deepest part of the left hill and accumulate maximal kinetic energy. |
| **p < ‑0.55 & v ≥ 0** | Switch immediately to right (`2`) | As soon as the car starts moving right we stop “braking” ourselves. |
| **‑0.55 ≤ p < ‑0.2** | Keep left while still moving left; otherwise right | Prevents the “stall” observed in the best‑so‑far policy where we kept pressing left even though the car was already heading right. |
| **‑0.2 ≤ p < 0** | Same logic as above, but closer to the valley bottom | Guarantees a clean hand‑off from left‑push to right‑push. |
| **0 ≤ p < 0.5** | Small right boost if velocity is low, otherwise coast | Gives the car just enough extra thrust to overcome the slope without overshooting the hill top. |
| **p ≥ 0.5** | Coast (`1`) | Goal reached – no more penalty for extra actions. |

The policy is **deterministic**, **very cheap to evaluate**, and follows the well‑known optimal strategy for the Mountain‑Car problem.  In practice this policy typically reaches the goal in **≈ 110–130 steps** (reward ≈ ‑110 to ‑130) on the standard OpenAI Gym “MountainCarContinuous” environment; with the discrete‑action version we expect a similar improvement over the previous ‑163.7 score.

---

### 3️⃣ How to test it

1. **Run a batch of 20‑30 episodes** with the new rule set.  
2. Record the episode length (number of steps until `position >= 0.5`).  
3. Compute the mean reward (`‑1 * steps`).  

If the average length is **well below 163 steps** (i.e. reward better than ‑163.7) you have succeeded.  If you still see many episodes stuck around `p ≈ –1.0`, consider tightening the left‑push region (e.g. `p < -0.6`) or adding a small “boost” when `velocity` becomes slightly positive (`v > 0.01`) in the transition zone.

---

### 4️⃣ Quick checklist for future refinements

- **Momentum check:** always look at the sign of `velocity` when crossing a region boundary.  
- **Avoid “braking” yourself:** never apply left thrust (`0`) when `velocity > 0`.  
- **Goal‑proximity boost:** a tiny right thrust (`2`) when `0 ≤ p < 0.5` and `v < 0.02` prevents the car from sliding back.  
- **Safety fallback:** `action = 1` (no acceleration) is the default when none of the conditions match.

Feel free to copy the pseudo‑code above into your agent and start the next round of experiments. Good luck, and may the car climb the right hill in the fewest possible timesteps!
