## 2. Why the best‑so‑far policy works (and where it fails)

| Situation | Rule that fires | What actually happens | Is the rule appropriate? |
|-----------|----------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|
| **Left of the valley bottom, moving left** (`position < 0` & `velocity < 0`) | `IF velocity < 0 → action = 0` (accelerate left) | Car gains more negative speed → good. | ✅ |
| **Left of the valley bottom, already moving right** (`position < 0` & `velocity ≥ 0`) | `IF position ≥ 0.0 THEN …` does **not** fire, so we fall through to the last `ELSE → action = 2` (accelerate right) | Car starts pushing right *while it is still far left*. This wastes the momentum that could have been used to go deeper left. | ❌ |
| **Just past the bottom (0 ≤ position < 0.5) with tiny positive velocity** (`velocity < 0.02`) | `IF position ≥ 0.0 AND velocity < 0.02 → action = 2` | Small right thrust pushes the car up the hill – OK, but the same rule also fires when the car is still at the bottom with *zero* velocity, causing a premature right thrust that slows the left‑ward swing. | ⚠️ |
| **Near the goal (position ≥ 0.5)** | `action = 1` (no acceleration) | Car stops receiving a penalty and the episode ends. | ✅ |

**Bottom line:** The policy is *almost* optimal, but it **starts pushing right too early** (while the car is still left of the valley bottom) and **never uses the “coast” action** when the car already has enough momentum to finish the climb.

